 ## Overview
-The NeRF MLP Architecture is a fully-connected deep neural network that represents a static scene as a continuous 5D volumetric function, mapping a 3D spatial location $\mathbf{x}$ and a 2D viewing direction $\mathbf{d}$ to volume density $\sigma$ and view-dependent emitted radiance $\mathbf{c}$ [S1]. It solves the problem of view synthesis by optimizing a compact neural representation directly from sparse input images, avoiding the need for explicit geometry reconstruction or large voxel grids [S1]. Unlike its siblings, it explicitly models view-dependent effects (such as specularities) via a separate direction-processing branch, and uses positional encoding to overcome the spectral bias of standard MLPs [S1].
+The NeRF MLP Architecture is a fully-connected deep neural network that represents a static scene as a continuous 5D volumetric function, mapping a 3D spatial location $\mathbf{x}$ and a viewing direction $\mathbf{d}$ to volume density $\sigma$ and view-dependent emitted radiance $\mathbf{c}$ [S1]. It solves the problem of view synthesis by optimizing a compact neural representation directly from sparse input images, avoiding the need for explicit geometry reconstruction or large voxel grids [S1]. Unlike its siblings, it explicitly models view-dependent effects (such as specularities) via a separate direction-processing branch, and uses positional encoding to overcome the spectral bias of standard MLPs [S1].
 
